summary:
Proves that a K3 surface over a number field with everywhere potentially good reduction has infinitely many reductions whose geometric Picard rank jumps (Theorem 1.1). Consequences:
- such an X_K̄ has infinitely many rational curves, unless X has infinitely many supersingular reductions (Corollary 1.3);
- abelian surfaces over O_K have infinitely many geometrically nonsimple reductions (Theorem 1.4);
- the same holds for the Kuga–Satake factors of Assumption 1.5 (Theorem 1.6) and for unitary (r, 1) abelian varieties, which acquire CM elliptic isogeny factors (Corollary 1.7).

All of these follow from Theorem 1.8. For a GSpin Shimura variety of a maximal lattice of signature (b, 2), b ≥ 3, a Hodge-generic O_K-point meets special divisors Z(m) at infinitely many primes; Theorem 2.4 does this with m ∈ D·□.

The proof compares two sides of the arithmetic height h_Ẑ(m)(Y):
- Howard–Madapusi Pera modularity gives h ≍ m^{b/2} (Proposition 3.2);
- Bruinier's explicit Green function gives an archimedean term ≍ −m^{b/2} log m outside a sparse set of m (Theorems 5.7, 6.1), using local densities, Heath-Brown's and Niedermowwe's circle method, and the diophantine bound of Theorem 5.8;
- the finite contribution at a fixed prime is o(X^{(b+1)/2} log X) on average over m ∈ D·□ (Theorem 7.1), from deformation-theoretic lattices of special endomorphisms and successive minima.

The libraries supply lattices, the discriminant form and the overlattice correspondence (Tau Ceti), and Clifford algebras, ₂F₁ and Gamma (Mathlib). The atlas plans the PEL moduli M(r, 1), Serre–Tate, Néron models and the Hodge index theorem.

Routes:
- the GSpin integral-model material goes to the orthogonal Part II of ShimuraVarieties proposed by PAPER-CHARLES-16;
- the heights, Green functions and counting argument go to a new Part II of GrossZagierAndArithmeticHeights;
- the K3 applications go to the K3 roadmap proposed by PAPER-CHARLES-16;
- general inputs go to GN.1/GN.3/GN.4, ES.3/ES.4, QM.3, R28.4 and AN.2 as sources.

Six source issues are recorded:
- for b odd, Lemma 5.3 and Proposition 5.2 are proved only for m ∈ D·□, while Theorems 5.7–5.8 are stated and used for all m (a repair is given);
- the §7.3 example's approximation gaps are too short;
- dim B = 2^n should be 2^{n−1};
- three groups of misprints and slips.

routes.brief:
This reuses the candidate id proposed by PAPER-CHARLES-16, whose brief covers the good-prime (p ≥ 5) orthogonal model and the Kuga–Satake map for K3 families. This paper needs more: the all-prime integral model of a GSpin Shimura variety and its special cycles. Start beyond Complex Shimura varieties and canonical models (ShimuraVarieties V0–V6), importing its analytic quotients and generic-fibre Hodge-type canonical models, Shimura data (ShimuraData D2–D5), Mathlib's CliffordAlgebra with its grading, even part and spinGroup, Tau Ceti's IntegralLattice (dual lattice, discriminant form and the even-overlattice/isotropic-subgroup correspondence), Abelian schemes and arithmetic moduli (AbelianSchemesAndArithmeticModuli A4 for Serre–Tate and Grothendieck–Messing, A5 for complex uniformization), Siegel and PEL moduli problems (PELModuli M1–M5), finite flat groups and Dieudonné theory (FiniteFlatGroupsAndIntegralPadicHodgeTheory R07.2, R07.6), Néron models (NeronModelsAndSemistableAbelianVarieties R11.1), and the integral Hodge-type models of the candidate Shimura varieties, Part II: integral models and reduction (ShimuraVarietiesHondaTatePartII) for Kisin's hyperspecial models.

Construct, for a maximal even lattice (L, Q) of signature (b, 2):
- GSpin(V), the spinor similitude, the datum (G, D_L) with reflex field Q, the level K = G(A_f) ∩ C(L̂)^× and M(C) = G(Q)\D_L × G(A_f)/K;
- the Kuga–Satake abelian scheme of relative dimension 2^{b+1} with its C(L)-action and grading, and the realizations V_B, V_dR, V_ℓ,ét, V_cris embedded in End_{C(L)}(H_?) with v ∘ v = Q(v);
- special divisors Z(β, m) over C and over Q, and Hodge-genericity.

The final theorems are:
- Proposition 2.1 exactly as stated: a flat normal Deligne–Mumford Z-stack, smooth at almost self-dual primes (self-dual, or p = 2 with dim V odd and 4 ∤ |L^∨/L|), carrying the extended Kuga–Satake scheme and ω, with the potentially-good-reduction extension property (AGHMP18 Prop. 4.4.1, Thm. 4.4.6);
- Definitions 2.2–2.3 with the auxiliary self-dual L^⋄ at bad primes and independence of L^⋄;
- the positive definite form on V(A_S);
- Madapusi Pera's isometries of V(A_x) ⊗ Q_ℓ and ⊗ Q_p with Tate classes (MP15 Thm. 6.4);
- the stacks Z(m), Z(β, m) with generic fibre Z(β, m) and étale-local Cartier property (AGHMP18 §4.5);
- Lemma 7.2: local intersection numbers are Σ_n #{v ∈ L_n : Q(v) = m};
- Proposition 7.3: rank bounds b + 2 and b, and L_{n_0′+ke} = (Λ + p^kL_{n_0′} ⊗ Z_p) ∩ L_{n_0};
- the supersingular characterization of rank L_n = b + 2, recorded as stated without proof;
- Lemma 7.10 on lifts Y_Λ of a canonical lift with prescribed Z_p-lattice Λ.

The application interfaces are:
- GSp_4 ≅ GSpin for b = 3 with the Kudla–Rapoport special endomorphisms s^† = s, tr s = 0;
- Assumption 1.5 and A^+ ~ B^{2^n}, where dim B = 2^{n−1}, not the printed 2^n;
- van Geemen's End(A^+_K̄) ⊗ Q = C^+(V) at Hodge-generic points;
- δ_0 = e_1⋯e_{b+2} and s = δ_0 ∘ v;
- the split example −x_1² − x_2² + Σx_i² + dx_{b+2}², b ≡ 3 mod 8;
- the unitary-to-orthogonal morphism G′ → SO(V) with (9.1).

Read AGHMP18 §4, MP16, MP15 §6, Kisin 2010, KR00, BHK+17 §2 and van Geemen before closing these nodes. Tests: a lattice not self-dual at 2 with dim V odd and 4 ∤ |L^∨/L| (smooth), one with 4 | |L^∨/L| (not claimed smooth); a point on Z(m) versus a Hodge-generic one; rank L_n ≤ b + 1 versus b + 2.

Start beyond Gross–Zagier formulas and arithmetic heights (GrossZagierAndArithmeticHeights). GZ.6 plans modularity of Chow-valued generating series on Shimura curves; this Part II does the same for GSpin Shimura varieties of signature (b, 2), b ≥ 3, and applies it to exceptional reductions.

Import:
- the orthogonal Part II (OrthogonalIntegralModelsAndKugaSatake) for M, the Kuga–Satake scheme, special endomorphisms, Z(m), Lemma 7.2 and Proposition 7.3;
- Arakelov geometry (ArakelovGeometryAndAbelianHeights R35.1) for hermitian line bundles and arithmetic degree;
- the Weil representation (MetaplecticAutomorphicForms MP.4) and harmonic Maass forms with Poincaré series (QSeriesPartitionsAndMockModularForms QM.3);
- local densities (GeometryOfNumbersAndQuadraticArithmetic GN.3), successive minima and lattice counts (GN.1, GN.4);
- Heath-Brown's and Niedermowwe's circle method (ExponentialSumsAndCircleMethod ES.3–ES.4);
- Mertens (AnalyticNumberTheory AN.2), Tate's isogeny theorem (FaltingsFinitenessAndIsogenyTheorems R28.4), and Mathlib's ₂F₁ and Gamma.

