Three corrections apply (sourceIssues E1–E3):
- For b odd, prove Lemma 5.3 and Proposition 5.2 for every m with Z(m) ≠ ∅, not only m ∈ D·□. Theorems 5.7–5.8 are stated, and used in Proposition 6.4 and Lemma 7.6, for all m. With d_0 varying, the functional equation of L(s, χ_{d_0}) cancels the log|d_0| terms of BK03 Thm. 4.11.
- Give the §7.3 approximation gaps length ≥ exp(exp(C·p^{2c(n+1)})), not exp(exp(p^n)), so that N_i ≥ e^{e^{D_i}} holds.
- dim B = 2^{n−1}.

Further requirements:
- Keep the normalization of E_0 (constant term 2) and of (3.1) against (8.2) (the 1/|Aut| weights) explicit.
- The b ≤ 2 cases of Theorem 1.8 are [Cha18, ST19] and remain imports. Do not claim Theorem 1.4 beyond what §9.2 proves plus those imports.
- Read HM17 §9, Bru02 Chs. 1–2, BK03 §§3–4, BK01, Nie10 and Tay20a Ex. 2.3 before closing.

Tests:
- the unimodular L = U² ⊕ E_8, where c(m) = −1008σ_5(m) = 2 × the E_6 coefficient;
- Lemma 4.11 for small b by direct integration;
- the boundedness of z^kG(k/2, z) + (b/2)log(1 − z) on [1/2, 1).

This reuses the new-roadmap candidate proposed by PAPER-CHARLES-16 for K3 geometry, adding an arithmetic endpoint. Import:
- Hodge structures (tauceti:TauCetiRoadmap/HodgeStructures) and Tau Ceti's IntegralLattice (unimodular even lattices, orthogonal complements, signature);
- intersection theory and the Hodge index theorem (SchemeAndStackFoundations SF.5);
- the orthogonal Part II (OrthogonalIntegralModelsAndKugaSatake) for M, the Kuga–Satake scheme, special endomorphisms and the extension property;
- the heights Part II (GSpinSpecialDivisorHeights) for Theorem 1.8.

Construct:
- the transcendental datum of a K3 surface over a number field: H² even unimodular of signature (3, 19), Pic ↪ H² primitive of signature (1, ρ − 1), and (L, −Q) of signature (b, 2) with b = 20 − ρ, with Hodge-genericity from Lefschetz (1, 1);
- André's results: the Kuga–Satake variety over K and its K-point (And96 Main Lemma 1.7.1), potentially good reduction from good reduction (Lemma 9.3.1), and special endomorphisms of A_𝔓 ↔ line bundles on X_𝔓 of self-intersection −m orthogonal to Pic(X) (Main Lemma 1.7.1(ii)).

The final theorems are:
- Theorem 1.1: a K3 surface over a number field with a smooth projective model over O_K after finite extension has ρ(X_𝔓̄) > ρ(X_K̄) for infinitely many 𝔓. The cases ρ ≥ 18 (b ≤ 2) come from Charles 2018 and Shankar–Tang, as the paper's footnote 1 says.
- Corollary 1.3: with potentially good reduction everywhere, either infinitely many rational curves on X_K̄ or infinitely many unirational (supersingular) specializations, via Li–Liedtke Prop. 4.2 and Thm. 4.3.

Read André 1996 §§1, 9 and Li–Liedtke 2012 §4 before closing. Tayou 2022 (Algebra Number Theory 19 (2025)) removes the good-reduction hypothesis and is a later extension, not part of this endpoint.

routes.reason:
GN.3 constructs local representation densities. This paper supplies explicit, uniform estimates for maximal lattices of rank ≥ 5:
- Hanke's Jordan splitting with ν_j ≤ 1 and solution types;
- the reduction maps (Lemma 4.2) and the closed formulas of Corollary 4.3;
- the stabilization and closeness-to-1 bounds (Lemmas 4.4–4.5), the lower bound 1/2 (Lemma 4.6), and the Bruinier–Kühn local term bound (Proposition 4.1);
- the discriminant-sensitive bound of Lemma 7.9.
They are general lattice statements, not specific to Shimura varieties.
